= Executive Summary
<executive-summary>
#strong[Email and identity are the core of every organisation\'s IT -- and the core of its dependency.] Whoever runs an
organisation\'s mailboxes and its accounts also runs the logins to every other service, the calendars, the contacts
and, usually, the files. Many organisations have handed that core to Microsoft or Google -- universities in the United
States, the United Kingdom and the Netherlands largely, those in Germany, France, Austria and Switzerland far
less#footnote[Fiebig et al., \"Heads in the Clouds? Measuring Universities\' Migration to Public Clouds\", Proceedings on Privacy Enhancing Technologies 2023(2) (MX records of universities), checked 2026-10-22: #link("https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.09462");];. Switching away is hard not
because good free software is missing, but because the free building blocks are separate systems: one for mail, one
for identity, one for files -- each with its own users, its own administration and its own operations.

#strong[kernoid is one system for that core.] kernoid is a self-hostable cloud application, written in Rust, that combines
an email and collaboration server with an identity provider and storage -- as one program with one data model:

- #strong[one identity] -- an account is created once and can be at the same time the mailbox, the login for single
  sign-on and the owner of files; nothing has to be synchronised;
- #strong[one administration] -- users, groups, domains and policies are managed in one place;
- #strong[one operation] -- one application to deploy, update and monitor, built for containers and Kubernetes, with
  PostgreSQL for data and S3-compatible object storage for messages and files.

#strong[Built on proven free software.] kernoid does not start from zero. It takes over what it can from two mature
projects under the GNU AGPL-3.0: from #strong[Stalwart];, a mail and collaboration server written in Rust (SMTP, IMAP, JMAP,
CalDAV, CardDAV, WebDAV)#footnote[Stalwart, README of `stalwartlabs/stalwart` (AGPL-3.0 and SELv2), checked 2026-10-22: #link("https://github.com/stalwartlabs/stalwart");];, and from #strong[Zitadel];, an identity provider with OpenID Connect, SAML 2.0,
SCIM 2.0 and passkeys#footnote[Zitadel, README of `zitadel/zitadel` (AGPL-3.0), checked 2026-10-22: #link("https://github.com/zitadel/zitadel");];. kernoid reuses Stalwart\'s AGPL-licensed code where it fits and carries Zitadel\'s
concepts and protocol logic over to Rust -- with the attribution both licences require.

#strong[Everything included, for everyone.] kernoid is free software under the GNU AGPL-3.0, and the free
release is the full product: single sign-on, provisioning, multi-tenancy and clustering are part of it -- the very
features that other projects reserve for a paid edition; Stalwart, for example, lists SCIM provisioning and
multi-tenancy with per-tenant identity backends as enterprise features#footnote[Stalwart Enterprise, feature list, checked 2026-10-22: #link("https://stalw.art/enterprise/");];. kernoid collects no telemetry.

#strong[A falkOSS project.] kernoid is published by falkOSS, a non-profit company for free software in formation in
Falkensee near Berlin #strong[\[OPEN: status of the incorporation and of the non-profit recognition at publication\]];.
falkOSS holds the name and funds the development; the technical direction lies with the project. As soon as kernoid
is stable, falkOSS will run its own email on it.

= The Problem
<the-problem>
== Whoever runs the core holds the keys
<whoever-runs-the-core-holds-the-keys>
Every other service of an organisation hangs on two things: the #strong[login];, which single sign-on hands out for all
applications, and the #strong[email address];, to which every account outside single sign-on sends its password resets.
Whoever operates mail and identity can let people in and lock them out -- everywhere at once.

That is not a theoretical risk. Providers based in the United States must hand over data on request \"regardless of
whether such communication, record, or other information is located within or outside of the United States\"#footnote[18 U.S.C. § 2713, added by the CLOUD Act (Pub. L. 115--141, div. V, 2018), checked 2026-10-22: #link("https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/2713");]
-- a European data centre does not change that. And in May 2025 the chief prosecutor of the International Criminal
Court lost access to his Microsoft email after the US sanctioned the court; Microsoft states that it never stopped its
services to the court itself#footnote[heise online, \"Criminal Court: Microsoft\'s email block a wake-up call for digital sovereignty\", 19 May 2025: #link("https://heise.de/-10387383");; heise online, \"Microsoft denies mail blockade at the International Criminal Court\", 5 June 2025, checked 2026-10-22: #link("https://heise.de/-10429628");];. Whatever happened in detail: an organisation that does not
run its core itself does not decide alone who keeps access to it.

== Free building blocks, separate systems
<free-building-blocks-separate-systems>
There is good free software for every part of the core -- mail servers, identity providers, file services. But each is
its own system with its own accounts. A self-hosted setup typically connects them: the mail server reads its users
from a directory over LDAP, accepts logins from an identity provider over OpenID Connect, or has its accounts created
and removed by that identity provider over SCIM. Stalwart, for example, offers all three ways next to its internal
directory#footnote[Stalwart documentation, authentication backends (internal; external LDAP, SQL, OIDC; SCIM provisioning), checked 2026-10-22: #link("https://stalw.art/docs/auth/backend/overview");];.

Connected systems are not one system:

- #strong[An account exists several times] -- in the identity provider, in the mail server, in the file service -- and
  every copy has to be created, changed and removed. If one connection fails, a person who left keeps a working
  mailbox, or a new colleague has none.
- #strong[Rights live in several places.] Who may read a shared mailbox, who may open a folder, who is administrator:
  each system answers that itself.
- #strong[Operation multiplies:] several administration interfaces, several update cycles, several backups, several
  sets of logs to look at when something goes wrong.

That is not a weakness of the individual projects. It follows from building the core out of separate systems.

== The connecting parts cost extra
<the-connecting-parts-cost-extra>
Many free projects finance themselves through a paid edition -- a legitimate model. But what moves into the paid
edition is often exactly what connects systems or serves many organisations at once:

- Stalwart lists SCIM provisioning and multi-tenancy with per-tenant identity backends as enterprise
  features#footnote[Stalwart Enterprise, feature list, checked 2026-10-22: #link("https://stalw.art/enterprise/");];.
- authentik reserves provisioning to Google Workspace and Microsoft Entra ID, among others, for its enterprise
  edition#footnote[authentik documentation, enterprise features, checked 2026-10-22: #link("https://docs.goauthentik.io/enterprise/enterprise-features/");];.

An organisation that wants to run its core seriously ends up paying in several places -- or living without these
features.

== Mail has to arrive
<mail-has-to-arrive>
A mail server can be configured perfectly and its messages still land in the spam folder. The large mailbox
providers set the rules: since February 2024 Google requires SPF or DKIM, valid reverse DNS and TLS from every
sender, and from bulk senders also DMARC and one-click unsubscribe#footnote[Google Workspace Admin Help, \"Email sender guidelines\", checked 2026-10-22: #link("https://support.google.com/a/answer/81126");];; Microsoft applies similar
rules to domains that send 5,000 or more messages to Outlook.com#footnote[Microsoft Support, \"Fix NDR error 550 5.7.515 in Outlook.com\", checked 2026-10-22: #link("https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/outlook/fix-ndr-error-550-5-7-515-in-outlook-com");];. Beyond those rules, a new server\'s address
has no sending history -- and reputation cannot be configured.

kernoid makes the configurable part the default and checks it: it shows when SPF, DKIM, DMARC, reverse DNS or
MTA-STS are missing or wrong. Reputation it cannot create -- but a new installation can send through a relay of the
operator\'s choice until its own address has built a history.

