== Managed service
<managed-service>
falkOSS does not sell kernoid as a service. A #strong[separate company] is planned to offer managed hosting
#strong[\[OPEN: name, timing\]];; any other provider can do the same. That company runs the same software under the same
licence and has no rights that other providers lack.

falkOSS itself hosts kernoid only for non-profit purposes -- and runs its own email on kernoid as soon as it is
stable.

= Roadmap
<roadmap>
The order is fixed; the timing is an aim, not a promise -- with fewer people or less money, it moves.

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 3,
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Phase], [Version], [Content],),
    table.hline(),
    [Pre-release], [0.1 (released 2026-10-28)], [identity and single sign-on, mail, calendars and contacts, files, the web app and a terminal app],
    [], [0.2], [the core completed: public links, resumable uploads, LDAP, the Kubernetes operator; end-to-end encryption; virus scanning and Jitsi as the first modules],
    [], [further 0.x], [WOPI, external key management, DNS automation, moving over; actions and hooks; the app for desktop and mobile; external security audit],
    [Stable], [1.0, once falkOSS is incorporated and holds the app stores\' developer accounts], [ready for production, with the app for desktop and mobile; falkOSS runs its own email, logins and files on it],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

Throughout the pre-release phase, kernoid\'s own apps are the #strong[web app] and the #strong[terminal app];; they are made as
good as the core. The #strong[app for desktop and mobile] is built alongside and released with 1.0 -- publishing it in the
app stores needs falkOSS as a legal entity with developer accounts. Everything else connects over the open
protocols.

== 0.1 -- the first pre-release
<01--the-first-pre-release>
- tenants, accounts, groups, roles, projects -- as events, with the audit trail as data
- login with MFA and passkeys; OpenID Connect and OAuth 2.0 for other applications; SAML 2.0 and OpenID Connect
  federation; SCIM 2.0
- mail: SMTP in and out, IMAP, JMAP, spam filtering, SPF, DKIM and DMARC
- calendars and contacts (CalDAV, CardDAV, JMAP); files with versions, sharing, team folders and WebDAV
- the web app with administration, the terminal app; one container image with Docker Compose, PostgreSQL and object
  storage

Pre-releases are for testing, not for production data.

== 0.2 -- the core completed, the first modules
<02--the-core-completed-the-first-modules>
- public links to files, uploads that resume after a broken connection
- LDAP for programs that need it; the open points of mail (SRS, sending as a group, OAUTHBEARER, IMAP `SORT` and
  `THREAD`)
- running in separate roles across several instances, with the Kubernetes operator
- #strong[end-to-end encryption] with OpenPGP -- mail, vaults (encrypted folders), calendars and address books -- in the
  web app and the terminal app (RFC 0006)
- #strong[virus scanning] of incoming mail and uploaded files, and #strong[Jitsi] -- video meetings with kernoid\'s login -- as the
  first modules
- what testing 0.1 turns up

Push notifications without any provider\'s account -- Web Push for the web app, UnifiedPush for Android -- follow in 0.x
(RFC 0007).

== Until stable
<until-stable>
The further modules come in this order (see Modules):

+ #strong[WOPI] -- editing documents where they are stored, with Collabora Online or ONLYOFFICE Docs.
+ #strong[External key management and DNS automation] -- the main key held in an external key store; DNS records for
  mail (DKIM, MTA-STS and others) set through the APIs of DNS providers.
+ #strong[Moving over] -- from Microsoft 365 and Google Workspace, including accounts and groups from Active Directory, and
  from any other service over IMAP and, where offered, CalDAV and CardDAV (for example Apple iCloud or GMX).

Alongside:

- the app for desktop and mobile, with end-to-end encryption from its first version (see Apps and protocol)
- actions and hooks for integrations (see Integrations)
- stable data formats and a documented upgrade path between versions
- documentation for operators, including moving over from other systems
- external security audit, results published

== 1.0 -- stable
<10--stable>
Ready for production, as soon as falkOSS is incorporated and holds the developer accounts the app stores require.
The app for desktop and mobile comes with it, and with it push notifications through Apple and Google -- set up by
each operator with their own keys or a relay of their choice; nothing is sent there by default.
falkOSS moves its own email, logins and files to kernoid.

== After 1.0
<after-10>
Without dates and in no fixed order: further integrations, an optional external search index.

= Get Involved
<get-involved>
kernoid is at its beginning. This is the moment when help shapes the most.

== Developers
<developers>
kernoid needs people who know or want to learn:

- #strong[Rust] -- async, networking, systems programming
- #strong[identity protocols] -- OpenID Connect, OAuth 2.0, SAML, SCIM, WebAuthn
- #strong[mail and groupware protocols] -- SMTP, IMAP, JMAP, CalDAV, CardDAV
- #strong[distributed systems] -- PostgreSQL, object storage, event sourcing
- #strong[apps] -- Flutter for desktop and mobile, TypeScript for the web
- #strong[operation] -- containers, Kubernetes, security

Every contribution is welcome -- code, review, documentation, translations, testing. Contributions are accepted under
the Developer Certificate of Origin.

